      "id": 1,
      "text": "Zillah takes its name from Zillah Oakes, the youngest daughter of Northern Pacific Railway president Thomas Fletcher Oakes, who helped finance the town’s canal.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zillah%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sprang to life in 1891 when the Sunnyside Canal brought irrigation water to the dry lower Yakima Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zillah%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s Teapot Dome Service Station, a quirky tribute to the 1920s scandal, earned a spot on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zillah%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Zillah officially became an incorporated city on January 5, 1911, solidifying its status amid the region’s growing agricultural boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zillah%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Nestled in Yakima County, Zillah is surrounded by lush orchards and vineyards that define the fertile Yakima Valley.",
